EXT. APARTMENT ROOFTOP - WATER TANK - DAY

Tar paper and heat shimmer. A wooden water tower hunches on iron legs. A rusted ladder climbs its side. Below, a city sprawls into haze.

DANTE, 17, in a stained T-shirt, scrapes flaking paint from a pipe with a putty knife. His brother REMY, 14, holds a bucket of sealant and a rag.

REMY
Dante.

DANTE
Yeah.

REMY
Can I try?

DANTE
You'll drop it.

REMY
I won't.

DANTE
You dropped the last one.

REMY
That was a wrench.

DANTE
It's the same thing.

Remy sets the bucket down. He plants his hands on his hips. Dante keeps scraping.

REMY
Dad said we could split it.

DANTE
Dad said I could handle it.

REMY
He said split.

DANTE
He said it to be nice.

REMY
Dante.

DANTE
Remy.

REMY
I'm fourteen.

DANTE
You're fourteen and a half.

REMY
That's what I said.

Dante sighs. He hands over the knife. Remy takes it, and the metal catches the sun. He crouches by the pipe, tongue between his teeth, and scrapes.

DANTE
Not that hard.

REMY
It's rusty.

DANTE
It's rusty, not stubborn.

REMY
You don't know that.

A chunk of paint falls away. Underneath, the pipe gleams, a bright silver gash. Remy beams.

REMY (CONT'D)
Look!

DANTE
Nice.

REMY
You didn't look.

DANTE
I looked.

REMY
You didn't.

Dante turns. The pipe shines. Something in his face changes. He reaches out, as if to touch it, and then the ladder rattles. Both freeze.

A head pokes over the edge of the roof: MRS. ALVAREZ, from 4C, an old woman in a housecoat, hauling a laundry basket.

DANTE (CONT'D)
(under his breath)
No.

REMY
She's going to see.

DANTE
She won't see.

REMY
She's looking right at it.

Mrs. Alvarez squints, adjusts her glasses, and sets the basket on the tar paper. She says nothing. Dante and Remy stare at her. She stares back, and then, slowly, she turns and begins to hang a towel on the line.

REMY (CONT'D)
(whispering)
She's not going to say anything.

DANTE
(whispering)
Don't talk.

They crouch together behind the tank. Remy hands Dante the knife. Dante hands Remy the rag. They go back to work, shoulder to shoulder, trading the tools without looking.

Dante drops the knife. It clatters. They both freeze again.

REMY
(grinning)
You dropped it.

DANTE
Shut up.

REMY
You dropped it, Dante.

DANTE
It slipped.

REMY
It slipped.

Dante shoves him. Remy shoves back. They tumble into the bucket, which tips, and sealant oozes across the roof in a black tongue. Mrs. Alvarez, at the line, calmly continues hanging her wash.

DANTE
Look what you did.

REMY
Look what you did!

They both scramble for the spill, rags and hands, then laugh, because there is nothing else to do.
